NAME
    cc - tiny C-subset compiler (AdventOS native)

SYNOPSIS
    cc FILE.c [-o OUT.elf]

DESCRIPTION
    A from-scratch single-file compiler that reads a C-subset source
    file and writes a runnable ELF32 binary for AdventOS. NOT a port
    of TinyCC. Same scope philosophy as lua(1) — pick a useful subset,
    write it directly, ship something that works today.

    Output ELF entry is at VA 0x40000000 (the same VA the kernel
    loader expects for every user binary). A compiled program can
    be invoked directly:

      cc hello.c              writes /hello.elf
      /hello.elf              runs it
      echo $?                 inspect exit status

IMPLEMENTED
    Types          int (signed 32-bit). char is recognized but
                   represents a byte only as the element type of
                   arrays/pointers — scalar `char x` is still int-sized.
                   Pointers: `int *p`, `char *p`. Arrays: `int x[N]`,
                   `char x[N]`.

    Operators      + - * / %    binary
                   == != < > <= >=    comparison
                   && ||  !     short-circuit boolean
                   & | ^ ~ << >>     bitwise
                   unary -      negation
                   unary &      address-of   (NAME only)         (session 92)
                   unary *      dereference                       (session 92)
                   a[i]         array/pointer indexing            (session 92)
                   'X' '\n'     char literal — lexes as T_NUM     (session 92)
                   c ? a : b    ternary                           (session 96)
                   += -= *= /= %=   compound assignment           (session 96)
                   ++name --name    prefix increment/decrement    (session 96)
                   name++ name--    postfix increment/decrement   (session 96)
                   Assignment is a statement, not an expression.

    Statements     int x;  int x = expr;       local declaration
                   char x;  char x = expr;     local declaration (session 92)
                   int *p;  char *p;           pointer declaration (session 92)
                   int a[N];  char a[N];       array declaration  (session 92)
                   name = expr;                 assignment
                   name op= expr;               compound assignment (session 96)
                                                  op is + - * / %
                   ++name;  name++;             increment statements (session 96)
                   --name;  name--;             decrement statements (session 96)
                   *p   = expr;                 store thru pointer (session 92)
                   a[i] = expr;                 store thru index   (session 92)
                   expr;                        expression-statement
                   if (e) stmt [else stmt]      conditional
                   while (e) stmt               loop
                   return [expr];               function return
                   { stmts }                    block

    Functions      int NAME(int p1, int p2, ...) { body }
                   Params may be int, char, int*, char*, or
                   struct T * (session 92, 97).
                   Forward references work — codegen patches
                   call sites after parsing.

    Structs        struct TAG { int x; char *s; struct TAG *next; };
                                                 (session 97)
                   struct TAG var;               struct value (local/global)
                   struct TAG *p;                pointer to struct
                   var.field = expr;             member assign
                   p->field   = expr;            arrow assign
                   var.field, p->field           member reads (rvalue)
                   &var                          address-of (yields T*)
                   Restrictions: every field is 4-byte (no byte-packed
                   char fields yet); struct typed fields must be
                   pointers (no nested struct values); only forward
                   references via pointers; no struct-by-value in
                   function params or returns.

    Preprocessor   #define NAME body        single-line object macros
                   #undef  NAME             remove a macro
                   #include "FILE"          relative paths resolve to /FILE
                   #ifdef NAME / #ifndef NAME / #else / #endif
                                            conditional compilation
                   (session 95)
                   The preprocessor runs as a pass over the source
                   before the lexer. Macro expansion is single-pass
                   text substitution — no rescanning, no function-
                   like macros, no line-continuation, no #if exprs.

    Globals        int x;  int x = N;  int *p;  int  a[N];
                   char c;             char *p; char a[N];
                   (session 93)
                   Top-level declarations live in a single .data
                   region appended to the binary. Initializers must
                   be integer literals (signed); arrays default to
                   all-zero. Functions read/write globals via
                   absolute-address moves (mov eax, [VA]).

    Intrinsics     sys_exit(code)              syscall 3, no return
                   sys_write(fd, addr, n)      syscall 12
                   sys_getpid()                syscall 2
                   print_int(n)                decimal + newline
                   puts(s)                     string + newline   (session 91)
                   print_str(s)                string, no newline (session 91)
                   printf(fmt_lit, ...)        formatted output   (session 94)

    printf format  %d  signed decimal int
                   %s  char* (NUL-terminated string)
                   %c  single byte
                   %x  lowercase hex (unsigned interpretation)
                   %%  literal %
                   The format string MUST be a literal — `printf(fmt, ...)`
                   where fmt is a variable won't compile. The format is
                   walked at compile time, and each %X expands to a
                   call to a fixed helper function. No padding/width
                   modifiers; no %f (no floats).

    Strings        "..." literals with escapes \n \t \r \0 \\ \" \'
                   Strings live in a pool appended to the code segment;
                   identical literals are deduplicated. Pool cap is 4 KiB
                   per program; up to 256 distinct literals.

CODEGEN
    Stack-based, EAX-as-result. Every expression computes its result
    into EAX. Multi-operand subexpressions push intermediate values
    to the stack and pop into EBX. Locals live on the stack at
    EBP-relative offsets. cdecl: args pushed right-to-left, caller
    cleans up.

    Not optimized — no constant folding, no register allocation
    past EAX/EBX, no peephole. Produces correct but bulky code.

DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED
    - variadic functions in general (printf is a compile-time
      intrinsic, not a real variadic function)
    - %f / floating-point — no FPU support in user code
    - width / precision modifiers in printf (e.g. %5d, %.3s)
    - function-like macros: #define ADD(a,b) ((a)+(b))
    - #if with arithmetic expressions (#ifdef / #ifndef only)
    - multi-line macro bodies (backslash continuation)
    - bitwise compound assignments (&=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=)
    - ++/-- on arr[i], *p, or expressions (NAME only)
    - assignment as an expression (a = b is a statement)
    - comma operator
    - struct value passing/returning (use pointers)
    - struct value assignment (use field-by-field)
    - char-sized struct fields (every field is 4 bytes)
    - struct nesting (only struct *T is allowed as a field type)
    - sizeof
    - union / enum / typedef
    - function pointers
    - multi-file compilation (linker)
    - compound assignment (+=, -=, ...)
    - ++ / --, ternary, comma operator
    - struct / union / enum / typedef
    - function pointers
    - multi-dimensional arrays / arbitrary-base indexing
      (a[i] only works when a is a NAME; `f()[i]` won't parse)
    - the linker step (multi-file compilation)

EXAMPLES
    Minimal program:
      int main() { return 42; }

    Recursion:
      int fact(int n) {
          if (n <= 1) return 1;
          return n * fact(n - 1);
      }
      int main() { print_int(fact(10)); return 0; }

    Hello world (session 91):
      int main() {
          puts("hello, world");
          return 0;
      }

    Sample at /hello.c demonstrates fact / sum / gcd.
    Sample at /strs.c demonstrates puts / print_str / escapes.
    Sample at /chars.c demonstrates char arrays, my_strlen, my_strcpy.
    Sample at /globs.c demonstrates global int/char arrays + initializers.
    Sample at /printf.c demonstrates %d %s %c %x %% formatting.
    Sample at /prep.c + /colors.h demonstrates #include + #define +
    #ifdef + #ifndef + #else + #endif + #undef.
    Sample at /ops.c demonstrates += -= *= /= %= ++ -- and ternary.
    Sample at /structs.c demonstrates struct point + a linked-list
    walk via struct node { int val; struct node *next; }.

EXIT STATUS
    0   compile succeeded
    1   parse error, lex error, or codegen overflow

SEE ALSO
    lua(1), sh(1), ed(1)

NOTES
    The compiler is single-pass with respect to functions: it parses
    the whole program, then emits code for every function and patches
    forward call sites in a fixups table. There is no IR — codegen
    walks the AST directly.

    Token cap is 2048 (NAME_MAX 24); code cap is 32 KiB. A program
    that exceeds either dies with "code section overflow" or
    "too many tokens". Bump the constants in user/cc.c if you need
    headroom — they're chosen to keep cc.bin around 137 KiB.
